GPIO WORKFLOW: PIN OWNERSHIP AND LIVE READ HAZARDS

GPIO device files expose hardware states, not ordinary disk bytes.
The exact available control commands depend on firmware version;
check 'help' and /dev for supported operations.

DISCOVERY
  pins
  ls /dev/gpio
  help gpio
  help pins

INTERACTIVE VS FINITE
  pins prints a finite pin map and exits; preferred via MCP.
  gpio is a full-screen UI and should run on a local TTY only.
  Reading a GPIO node with an unbounded cat may repeatedly return
  the same digit forever; this is normal for a live level source.
  hexdump requires lseek if given an offset, which some device
  endpoints reject. Do not assume 'hexdump /dev/gpio/25 0 1' works.

CONTROL CHECKLIST
  1. Confirm connector pin mapping and voltage domain.
  2. Identify current owner in pins (fb, UART, PWM, I2C, etc.).
  3. Decide desired direction, value and maximum duration.
  4. Obtain authorization for changes.
  5. Use a documented command or device control operation.
  6. Recheck pins and external hardware status; restore if needed.

PIN EXAMPLES
  GP16/17: input switches with pull-ups on teaching board.
  GP18: connected buzzer; GP19: connected servo motor. Both share
  PWM slice 1 frequency. Do not repurpose either as a spare GPIO
  while the attached hardware is in use.
  GP20/21: UART1. GP26/27: I2C1.
  GP25: board LED on standard Pico; board variations matter.

No generic write command is given here: a pin's direction and
hardware owner must be established before constructing an action.
